  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs an upper level shortwave passes to our east, a high pressure \nsystem builds into the region this afternoon. With heights rising \nand the NAEFS showing height anomalies +2.5-3.0 STDEVs for today, \nhigh temperatures will be warm ranging from around 70F in the W. \nAdirondacks to mid 80s in the mid Hudson Valley. However the second \nhalf of the afternoon will see lowered dewpoints in the 50s-60s \nbehind a passing cold front allowing for comfortable humidity \nlevels. Cold air advection from the front and strong upper level \nwinds seen in forecast soundings will encourage further mixing down \nto the surface of gusty winds from the W-NW. Wind gusts in the 25-35 \nmph range could develop through the evening today and could blow \naround any outdoors unsecured objects. Drier air continues to filter \nin tomorrow indicating a favorable environment for radiational \ncooling tomorrow night, where terrain areas, especially in the \nW. ADKs could see overnight lows in the 40s, with low 50s in \nvalleys.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs the high pressure starts to shift east off the coast Saturday \nafternoon, a warmer more southerly flow will begin to transfer \nincreased moisture into the region, increasing dewpoints to more \nnoticeable levels in the 60s. NAEFS guidance show PWAT values \nabout +1.5-2\" STDEV. Models suggest scattered rain showers and \nisolated thunderstorms during two periods from Saturday evening \ninto Sunday. The first chance is timed with a passing short wave\nnear the coast Saturday evening where much of the available \nmoisture is focused. The best chance for scattered showers will \nbe in areas south and east of Albany, especially across Ulster, \nDutchess, and Litchfield counties. Skinny vertical profiles have\nwarm cloud layer depths greater than 10,000 ft which will \nlikely support efficient warm rain processes. A rumble of \nthunder Saturday night cannot be ruled out as low amounts of \ninstability exists within the warm sector where SBCAPE values \nrange 500-1000 J/Kg. \n\nProbabilistic guidance has a 30-40% chance for areas south and \neast of the Capital Region to receive or exceed 0.5\" and a low \nchance (less than 20%) to receive 1\" or greater. However some \nuncertainty still exists at this time as there is some \ndisagreement between models as to rainfall totals in these \nareas. There is higher confidence that areas north and west of \nAlbany will receive less than 0.5\". Across the region, rainfall\ntotals in the 0.25-0.50\" range are expected. Additional \nisolated to scattered showers could linger into early Sunday \nmorning where there is terrain enhancement, mainly in western \nNew England. A few thunderstorms are then possible on Sunday \nahead of an approaching cold front with the best chance \nnorthwest of Albany where SBCAPE values are near 1500 J/kg. WPC \nhas placed just the very southern parts of the CWA in a Marginal\nRisk (level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall Saturday through \nSunday night where localized flooding primarily effecting urban \nareas and low lying areas where poor drainage flooding typically\noccurs. Drier and seasonable temperatures then build in for \nearly next week.&&",
